SAMPLE ===== oblique (((x)^(2) + 5))/((x + 2))
SAMPLE [heading] The function
SAMPLE   {f(x) = (((x)^(2) + 5))/((x + 2))}
SAMPLE Draw the whole graph — every x where it is defined.
SAMPLE [heading] Step 1 · Domain and asymptotes
SAMPLE a denominator must not be 0.  So x ≠ −2.
SAMPLE [subheading] Vertical asymptotes
SAMPLE   x = −2 is not in the domain, and next to it |f(x)| grows without bound — a vertical asymptote.
SAMPLE     [result] x → −2⁻:  f(x) → −∞;    x → −2⁺:  f(x) → +∞
SAMPLE [subheading] Behaviour at the ends of the domain
SAMPLE   Divide: (x² + 5)/(x + 2) = x − 2 + 9/(x + 2). The last part → 0 as x → ±∞, so the graph approaches the line y = x − 2 — an oblique asymptote.
SAMPLE [heading] Step 2 · Intercepts with the axes
SAMPLE [subheading] y-axis (x = 0)
SAMPLE   f(0) = 2.5  →  the point (0, 2.5)
SAMPLE [subheading] x-axis: solve f(x) = 0
SAMPLE   A fraction is 0 when its numerator is 0. The denominator x + 2 is not 0 in the domain, so solve x² + 5 = 0:
SAMPLE   x² + 5 = 0:  Δ = b² − 4ac = 0² − 4·1·5 = −20
SAMPLE     Δ < 0, so there are no real solutions.
SAMPLE   [result] No solutions on its domain.
SAMPLE [heading] Step 3 · Extreme points
SAMPLE [subheading] The derivative
SAMPLE   Differentiate and factor:  {f′(x) = ((((x)^(2) + 4*x) - 5))/(((x + 2))^(2))}
SAMPLE [subheading] Solve f′(x) = 0
SAMPLE   Quotient rule: f′(x) = [2x(x + 2) − (x² + 5)]/(x + 2)² = (x² + 4x − 5)/(x + 2)²
SAMPLE   A fraction is 0 when its numerator is 0. The denominator (x + 2)² is positive wherever f is defined, so solve x² + 4x − 5 = 0:
SAMPLE   x² + 4x − 5 = 0:  Δ = b² − 4ac = 4² − 4·1·(−5) = 36
SAMPLE     x = (−b ± √Δ)/(2a) = (−4 ± √36)/2  ⇒  x = 1  or  x = −5
SAMPLE   [result] On its domain:  x = −5,  x = 1
SAMPLE [subheading] Sign of f′ — the proof
SAMPLE   f′ can only change sign where it is 0 or undefined (at the asymptotes), so one test point in each part decides the sign there:
SAMPLE   −∞ < x < −5:   f′(−7) ≈ 0.64 > 0   ↗ increasing
SAMPLE   −5 < x < −2:   f′(−4) ≈ −1.25 < 0   ↘ decreasing
SAMPLE   −2 < x < 1:   f′(−1) = −8 < 0   ↘ decreasing
SAMPLE   1 < x < ∞:   f′(3) ≈ 0.64 > 0   ↗ increasing
SAMPLE [subheading] Conclusion
SAMPLE   [result] x = −5: f′ changes from + to −  ⇒  maximum at (−5, −10).
SAMPLE     Check: f″(−5) ≈ −0.667 < 0 (∩) ✓
SAMPLE   [result] x = 1: f′ changes from − to +  ⇒  minimum at (1, 2).
SAMPLE     Check: f″(1) ≈ 0.667 > 0 (∪) ✓
SAMPLE [heading] Step 4 · Inflection points
SAMPLE [subheading] The second derivative
SAMPLE   Differentiate f′ again:  {f″(x) = (18)/(((x + 2))^(3))}
SAMPLE [subheading] Solve f″(x) = 0
SAMPLE   f″(x) = 18/(x + 2)³ = 0 has no solution: the top is never 0.
SAMPLE   [result] No solutions on its domain.
